  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0000-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: We'll hear argument now in No. 20 - 02-1060, Illinois v. Robert S. Lidster."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Illinois",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "A Lombard informational checkpoint was designed to find witnesses to a specific known crime. The principal question here is whether the checkpoint is per se invalid under the Fourth Amendment or whether it's governed by the Brown balancing test."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, I suppose we'd have to decide here not only whether it was per se invalid, but whether it was reasonable under any other standard."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Illinois",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would rely on the amicus briefs submitted by the Illinois Association of Chiefs of Police and the Major Cities Chiefs Association, and they - they have informed the Court that roadblocks are not used frequently, rather they are used judiciously in order to solve certain -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Illinois",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We would say that such roadblocks should not be declared per se invalid under the Fourth Amendment, but rather they ought to be subject to the Brown balancing test."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF PATRICIA A. MILLETT ON BEHALF OF THE UNITED STATES AS AMICUS CURIAE SUPPORTING THE PETITIONER"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But can you stop the person? I mean, put it in a pedestrian context, do you think a police officer can insist that the person stop, stand there, and listen to his pitch?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: I have a pending question about the - the third reason. I - what's the third reason?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Donald J. Ramsell (Respondent)",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. RAMSELL: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The mass, suspicionless seizure of innocent citizens for the purpose of investigating ordinary criminal wrongdoing without exigent circumstances is a violation of the Fourth Amendment, and Indianapolis v. Edmond is the controlling precedent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Mr. Ramsell, did I understand your argument to be that if the crime had been a child abduction, that this kind of roadblock to try to get evidence would be permissible?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Ramsell",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. RAMSELL: Justice O'Connor, my understanding is that the roadblock itself was entirely ineffective. It had zero effectiveness in seeking to obtain information regarding this event."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Ramsell",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. RAMSELL: To be frank, searching the record it's - it's relatively unclear. My understanding is 90 minutes to 2 hours at most."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Ramsell",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0002-0003",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. RAMSELL: I would believe that it would be more - more likely for abuse if this Court were to suggest that these type of roadblocks were a valid investigative tool."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Are there indications nationwide that the public makes objection to roadblocks, other than the person that's caught for drunk driving?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Gary Feinerman)",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This Court in Edmond found that narcotics check - narcotics checkpoints are so obnoxious to core Fourth Amendment values that a per se rule of invalidity was justified and the principal question here is whether informational checkpoints present the same"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.7,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "uncertain",
      "text": "QUESTION: Thank you, Mr. Ramsell."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Gary Feinerman)",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The first is that it's the purpose of the checkpoint to incriminate the motorist as opposed to the situation in Lombard, which was simply the police asking for help, and that distinction makes a difference under the Fourth Amendment for several reasons."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2003-02-1060",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ef009d965fd831c495a073d1eddc5e121823932209579a2f4b0819b98dca5b9b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ef009d965fd8-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: Thank you, Mr. Feinerman. The case is submitted."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
